        "prefix_token_count": 86,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: First, simplify the congruence equation \\(14u \\equiv 46 \\pmod{100}\\) by dividing all terms by their greatest common divisor (GCD). The GCD of 14, 46, and 100 is 2. Dividing through by 2 gives \\(7u \\equiv 23 \\pmod{50}\\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 375,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 377,
        "char_start": 229,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 136,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 137,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: To solve for \\(u\\), we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 7 modulo 50. We seek an integer \\(x\\) such that \\(7x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{50}\\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 622,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 624,
        "char_start": 379,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 226,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 227,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the inverse. Applying the algorithm:\n\\[50 = 7 \\cdot 7 + 1\\]\n\\[1 = 50 - 7 \\cdot 7\\]\nThis shows that the inverse of 7 modulo 50 is -7, which is equivalent to 43 modulo 50 since \\(-7 + 50 = 43\\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 767,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 768,
        "char_start": 626,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 285,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 286,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: Multiply both sides of the simplified congruence \\(7u \\equiv 23 \\pmod{50}\\) by 43 to isolate \\(u\\):\n\\[u \\equiv 23 \\cdot 43 \\pmod{50}\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 908,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 910,
        "char_start": 770,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 362,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 363,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: Calculate \\(23 \\cdot 43 \\mod 50\\):\n\\[23 \\cdot 43 = 989\\]\n\\[989 \\div 50 = 19 \\text{ remainder } 39\\]\nSo, \\(989 \\equiv 39 \\pmod{50}\\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1040,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1042,
        "char_start": 912,
        "effective_step": 6,
        "end_token_index": 412,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 413,
        "step_number": 6,
        "text": "Step 6: Therefore, \\(u \\equiv 39 \\pmod{50}\\). This means the general solution for \\(u\\) is \\(u = 39 + 50k\\) for any integer \\(k\\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1192,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1194,
        "char_start": 1044,
        "effective_step": 7,
        "end_token_index": 473,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 474,
        "step_number": 7,
        "text": "Step 7: Find the two smallest positive integer solutions by setting \\(k = 0\\) and \\(k = 1\\):\n- For \\(k = 0\\), \\(u = 39\\).\n- For \\(k = 1\\), \\(u = 89\\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1334,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1335,
        "char_start": 1196,
        "effective_step": 8,
        "end_token_index": 523,
        "near_end": true,
        "prefix_token_count": 524,
        "step_number": 8,
        "text": "Step 8: Calculate the average of these two smallest positive integer solutions:\n\\[\\text{Average} = \\frac{39 + 89}{2} = \\frac{128}{2} = 64\\]"
      }
